
======================================================================================================================
k_kernel-shape_qaniso  --  candidate K4:  Q = gamma_perp/gamma_par, the EFE anisotropy ratio
======================================================================================================================
  COMPUTE stage, angle 'kernel-shape'.  Independent recomputation of the proposed candidate, plus the
  three dilutions the proposing script omits (internal field, orbit averaging, mass-dependent regime).

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
0.  RESTATEMENT TEST -- executed, not asserted:  can Q be derived from v^4 = G M_b a0 ?
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Step 1.  Write down everything the deep-MOND relation asserts:  v^4 = G M_b a0, for an ISOLATED system
           in the limit g_bar << a0.  It contains M_b, v and a0.  It contains no external field, no
           direction, and no second body's orientation.
  Step 2.  Attempt the derivation.  Q is a ratio of the system's response to a perturbation ALONG g_ext
           to its response PERPENDICULAR to g_ext.  Take the deep-MOND relation and differentiate it with
           respect to an external field:  d(v^4)/d g_ext = 0 identically, because g_ext does not appear.
           The derivation therefore produces NO number for Q -- not Q = 1, but no statement at all.
  Step 3.  Confirm numerically that the asymptotic relation is blind to the kernel shape that sets Q:
      alpha=0.4                     nu(y) sqrt(y) at y = 1e-40, 1e-60, 1e-80 :  1.0000000125 1.0000000000 1.0000000000   (at y = 1e-8 it is 1.031829)
      alpha=1 (Route A)             nu(y) sqrt(y) at y = 1e-40, 1e-60, 1e-80 :  1.0000000000 1.0000000000 1.0000000000   (at y = 1e-8 it is 1.000050)
      alpha=3                       nu(y) sqrt(y) at y = 1e-40, 1e-60, 1e-80 :  1.0000000000 1.0000000000 1.0000000000   (at y = 1e-8 it is 1.000000)
      sqrt 1+1/y (frozen amdt2)     nu(y) sqrt(y) at y = 1e-40, 1e-60, 1e-80 :  1.0000000000 1.0000000000 1.0000000000   (at y = 1e-8 it is 1.000000)
      simple                        nu(y) sqrt(y) at y = 1e-40, 1e-60, 1e-80 :  1.0000000000 1.0000000000 1.0000000000   (at y = 1e-8 it is 1.000050)
      (the ASYMPTOTE is alpha-free; the APPROACH is not, which is the honest caveat -- at y = 1e-8 the
       alpha = 0.4 kernel is still 3.2% off the deep-MOND limit.  Real systems never reach y = 1e-40.)
  [PASS] 0a  the deep-MOND limit v^4 = G M_b a0 is satisfied IDENTICALLY by every kernel in the class, so it carries zero information about the shape constant that sets Q
         (max |nu sqrt(y) - 1| over five kernels at y <= 1e-40 is 1.25e-08; the relation cannot be a source for Q)
  VERDICT on the restatement test:  the derivation does NOT close.  is_restatement = FALSE.
  (Reported against interest: 'not a restatement' is a necessary condition and not a sufficient one --
   a quantity can fail to be a restatement and still fail to be measurable, which is what happens below.)

